---
title: Transparent Use of County Resources
order: 4
icon: scale
---

Corruption has bled Nairobi County dry. Billions of shillings meant for roads, schools, clinics, and water projects have disappeared into the pockets of connected contractors and compromised officials. Audit reports gather dust on shelves while residents of Kahawa West and Dandora navigate cratered roads and wait for services that were budgeted and supposedly delivered. The county's budget process is opaque, procurement is rigged, and the public is locked out of decisions that affect their daily lives.

We demand a participatory budgeting process that begins at the ward level. Every financial year, the county must hold public budget hearings in every ward before the County Assembly approves the estimates. Citizens must see — in plain language, not bureaucratic jargon — how much money is allocated to each project, who is implementing it, and by when it will be completed. All county procurement documents — tender notices, bid evaluations, contracts, and completion certificates — must be published on a public portal within seven days of award.

An independent county audit office, reporting directly to the public and not to the county executive, must conduct quarterly audits and publish findings within 30 days of the end of each quarter. We will establish a County Oversight Committee composed of civil society representatives, professional bodies, and religious leaders, with the power to summon county officials and compel production of documents. Whistleblowers who expose corruption must be protected by county law, not victimized.

The Nairobi County government spends your money. You have a right to know how. We will enact a County Transparency Ordinance that makes proactive disclosure the default — budgets, expenditures, contracts, project status, payroll, and development plans all available online in open, machine-readable formats. A transparent county is a functional county. A corrupt county is a failed county. Under Dr. Bill Llia's leadership, Nairobi will choose transparency.
